{\bf 1. Introduction}

The study0 of the quantum Hall effect (QHE) has been one of the most active areas of research in condensed matter physics over the last three decades. The QHE is a quantum mechanical phenomenon that occurs in two-dimensional electron systems subjected to a strong magnetic field. It is characterized by plateaus in the Hall conductance at integer multiples of $e^2/h$, where $e$ is the electron charge and $h$ is Planck's constant. The QHE is a topological phase of matter, which is robust against disorder and impurities. The edge states in the QHE are chiral and protected by time-reversal symmetry. These edge states are responsible for the quantization of the Hall conductance.

The QHE has been observed in various materials, including GaAs/AlGaAs heterostructures, graphene, and topological insulators. In GaAs/AlGaAs heterostructures, the QHE was first discovered by von Klitzing et al. in 1980 \cite{klitzing}. Since then, it has been studied extensively both experimentally and theoretically. The QHE in graphene was discovered by Novoselov et al. in 2005

