{\bf 1. Introduction}

The study0 of the quantum Hall effect (QHE) has been one of the most active areas of condensed matter physics over the past three decades. The QHE is a topological phenomenon that occurs in two-dimensional electron systems subjected to a strong magnetic field, where the Hall conductance exhibits plateaus at integer multiples of $e^2/h$ \cite{klitzing}. The QHE is a direct manifestation of the quantization of the Chern number, which is a topological invariant characterizing the ground state wave function of the system \cite{thouless}. The Chern number is defined as the integral of the Berry curvature over the Brillouin zone, and it is closely related to the Thouless-Kohmoto-Nightingale-Nijs (TKNN) formula \cite{tknn}, which gives the Hall conductance in terms of the Berry curvature.

The TKNN formula was originally derived using the Kubo formula for linear response theory. However, it can also be derived from the adiabatic theorem, which states that the ground state wave function of a gapped system remains unchanged under slow changes of the Hamiltonian if the gap does not close during the process \cite{adiabatic}. This

