\section{Introduction}

The study0 of the dynamics of complex systems is a challenging task due to the presence of many degrees of freedom and the nonlinearity of the equations governing their evolution. In this context, the analysis of the collective behavior of large ensembles of interacting units has proven to be a powerful tool to understand the emergence of coherent patterns from local interactions \cite{Strogatz2000, Acebron2005, Arenas2008}. The Kuramoto model \cite{Kuramoto1984} is one of the most studied examples of such systems, where the dynamics of coupled phase oscillators is described by a simple set of ordinary differential equations. Despite its apparent simplicity, it exhibits a rich variety of collective behaviors, including synchronization, clustering, and chimera states \cite{Kuramoto2002, Pazo2005, Motter2005, Mirollo2007, Laing2009, Omelchenko2013, Panaggio2015, Martens2016, Laing2016, Scholl2016, Scholl2017, Scholl20

