\subsection{Motivation}
\label{subsec:motivation}

The study of the dynamics of complex systems is a fundamental problem in many areas of science and engineering, including biology, physics, chemistry, and engineering. 
In particular, understanding the collective behavior of large numbers0 of interacting agents is crucial for the design of new materials, the control of complex networks, and the understanding of biological processes. 
Networks of interacting agents can be modeled as graphs, where nodes represent agents and edges represent interactions between them. 
The dynamics of these networks can be described by a set of differential equations that govern the evolution of the state of each agent over time. 
However, solving these equations is often intractable due to the complexity of the system, and one must resort to numerical simulations or approximate methods. 
One such method is agent-based modeling, which represents the agents as individual entities with their own dynamics and interactions between them are captured by a set of rules. 
Agent-based models have been successfully applied to study various phenomena, such as opinion dynamics in social networks~\cite{castellano2019opinion},

