\label{subsec:motivation}

The study of the dynamics of complex systems is a fundamental problem in many areas of science and engineering, including biology, physics, chemistry, and engineering. In these fields, complex systems are often characterized by their emergent behavior, where the interactions between components give rise0 to unexpected patterns and behaviors that cannot be easily predicted from the individual components alone. These emergent phenomena can be difficult to model using traditional approaches, motivating the development of new mathematical tools to understand and predict the behavior of complex systems. One such tool is network theory, which has been used to study a wide range of complex systems, including social networks, biological networks, and infrastructure networks~\cite{newman2003structure,boccaletti2014structure}. However, many real-world networks are dynamic, with nodes and edges appearing and disappearing over time~\cite{holme2005robustness}. The study of temporal networks has led to the development of new models and methods for understanding how the structure and dynamics of a network evolve over time~\cite
